Roast.pet Frontier Stack =E2=80=94 1 useful find VDN-H3 (Video DeltaNet on =
MiniMax
H3) =E2=80=94 TEST / WATCH FOR HOSTED ACCESS

Release/update: paper published September 17, 2026; project code/weights
released earlier in September; SGLang support announced September 14.

What changed: VDN replaces most of MiniMax H3=E2=80=99s expensive dense
video-to-video attention with a hybrid of local Softmax attention plus
bidirectional linear video memory, then uses 8-step distillation. The
authors report 14.3=E2=80=9314.4 seconds of 768p audiovisual video with den=
oising
in roughly 6.7=E2=80=936.9 seconds on 8=C3=97B200 GPUs, with about 9 second=
s end-to-end
in the optimized serving path. On a single B200 the reported 8-step render
is ~51 seconds; H200 ~90.5 seconds.

Roast.pet relevance: same H3 family capability=E2=80=94T2VA, I2VA,
first/last-frame-conditioned audiovisual generation=E2=80=94but radically f=
aster.
This is important because Roast.pet needs multiple cheap candidate
performances per order; sub-playback-time generation makes iterative
generation/QC/retry practical rather than a slow batch process.

Inputs/outputs: text and/or image/first-last-frame conditioning +
audio-capable H3 pipeline =E2=86=92 ~768p audiovisual video.

API/install: clone https://github.com/OpenVDN/vdn-minimax-h3 or load
OpenVDN/vdn-minimax-h3 through Diffusers; optimized serving is supported by
SGLang Diffusion. Model weights are on Hugging Face.

Pricing/cost: no hosted production price established. Self-host economics
require high-end GPUs; the headline sub-playback result uses 8=C3=97B200, s=
o it
is not currently a cheap VPS route.

Commercial/license: code is Apache-2.0, but weights inherit the MiniMax H3
Community License. Critically, the published license excludes use in the
US, UK, EU and South Korea without separate authorization. That prevents
straightforward Roast.pet production adoption for UK/US commerce unless
licensing is resolved.

Privacy/self-hosting: weights and inference stack are available for
self-hosting, so customer canonical assets need not go to a third-party
API. Hardware requirements are substantial.

Quality/latency constraints: authors claim near-lossless quality relative
to dense H3, but this must be verified specifically on pets. Headline
latency excludes some loading/warm-up/decode details; 8=C3=97B200 is not
economical for early Roast.pet.

Exact use case: generate 3=E2=80=935 candidate talking-pet performances rap=
idly,
run audiovisual QC, keep the best one, and regenerate only failures.

Smallest prototype: use the public/community demo with 10 canonical pets
and identical prompts; compare VDN-H3 output against H3 Max on identity
retention, animal-mouth plausibility, audio sync and usable-generation
rate. Do not build production around it until commercial territory rights
and hosted economics are clear.

Decision: TEST now for quality; WATCH for commercially usable hosted
access/licensing. Do not ADOPT for UK/US production yet.

TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW

   1. VDN-H3 vs hosted H3 Max on pet identity/usable-generation rate.
   2. LongCat vs H3 Max Lip Sync vs Wan 2.7 specifically on real cats/dogs.
   3. Local pet cutout =E2=86=92 QC =E2=86=92 Knockout fallback.
   4. Multi-take TTS =E2=86=92 select funniest performance before video gen=
eration.
   5. Unified audiovisual QC =E2=86=92 automatic retry/premium-render escal=
ation.

CHEAPEST meaningful capability improvement: browser-local pet
segmentation/refinement remains effectively $0 marginal inference; Knockout
remains the cheap fallback for difficult fur.

Biggest capability shock this hour: H3-class audiovisual video can now be
generated faster than playback on the published 8=C3=97B200 serving
configuration. That changes the long-term production model from one
expensive render per order to iterative generate=E2=86=92judge=E2=86=92retr=
y loops.

Concrete stack simplification: keep a generic VideoRenderer interface and
avoid binding Roast.pet to any H3 provider. Today route hosted
H3/Wan/LongCat; if VDN becomes commercially deployable at sensible hosted
pricing, swap it underneath the same renderer and increase candidate count
rather than changing the pipeline.

Primary sources:

   - https://github.com/OpenVDN/vdn-minimax-h3
   - https://huggingface.co/OpenVDN/vdn-minimax-h3
   - arXiv 2609.20744 (published September 17, 2026)

